---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

// Boilerplate copy; the wording gets a proper pass with the SEO work.
const faqs = [
	{
		q: "Is LanternCert really free?",
		a: "Yes. Every question, guide and practice test is free, with no account, no paywall and no ads. There is no paid tier waiting behind the free one.",
	},
	{
		q: "Do I need to sign up?",
		a: "No. There is nothing to sign up for. You can start a practice test the moment you land on your state's page.",
	},
	{
		q: "Where does your information come from?",
		a: "From the agency that publishes it - your state's licensing agency, its handbook, or the statute itself. Every fact on a state page links to the document it came from, and we record the date we last checked it.",
	},
	{
		q: "How do I know it is still current?",
		a: "Each state page shows the date its facts were last validated against their sources, and each source shows the year it was published and the year we last confirmed it is still the operative version.",
	},
	{
		q: "Are your practice questions the real exam questions?",
		a: "No. No legitimate site has the real question bank. Ours are written fresh from each state's own material to cover the same ground the real exam covers.",
	},
	{
		q: "Which states do you cover?",
		a: "Florida, Georgia, Texas and California to start. More states are being added, and the site is built so a new one drops in without rework.",
	},
	{
		q: "Can I use this instead of my state's rider course?",
		a: "No. LanternCert is study material, not a substitute for any required course, and it is not legal advice. Always confirm what your state requires with your state's licensing agency.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="What LanternCert is, how our study material is sourced and kept current, and how we handle your data."
>
	<div class="stack-lg">
		<div class="stack">
			<h1 class="page-title">About LanternCert</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				LanternCert is a free, no-signup place to learn everything you need to pass a certification
				exam - starting with motorcycle endorsements.
			</p>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Most study sites for exams like these are either covered in ads or hidden behind a
					subscription, and almost none of them tell you where their information came from. That is a
					problem when the answer changes by state, and changes again when an agency updates a fee or
					a handbook.
				</p>
				<p>
					So we do it differently. Every requirement, cost and exam detail on this site is tied to
					the document it came from - a state handbook, an agency page, or the statute itself - and
					every one of those links is on the page for you to follow. We record the year each document
					was published, the year we last confirmed it is still the current version, and the date we
					last checked our own facts against it.
				</p>
				<p>
					The practice questions are written from those same documents, question by question, so what
					you study is what your state actually tests. No ads, no signups, no upsell.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<section class="stack" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
			<h2 class="section-title" id="faq-heading">Common questions</h2>
			<div class="faq">
				{faqs.map((faq) => (
					<details class="faq__item">
						<summary>{faq.q}</summary>
						<p>{faq.a}</p>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="stack" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading" id="privacy">
			<h2 class="section-title" id="privacy-heading">Privacy</h2>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					We do not ask for your name, your email or any other personal information, because there is
					nothing here to sign up for. Anything you do on the site - the questions you have answered,
					your test results, the certifications you have starred - is saved only in your own
					browser's local storage on the device you are using, never sent to us, and never sold or
					shared with anyone. You can download or erase it yourself at any time from your
					<a href="/progress">progress page</a>, and clearing your browser data removes it as well.
					We use Google Analytics to see which
					pages and questions get used, which tells us what to fix and what to write next; that
					measurement is anonymous and is never tied to an individual person.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>
